# Every artifact gate in `api/tests`, classified by WHERE it is filed and — when that is away from the
# category home — by what it is mirrored on.
#
# An artifact gate is a kernel-free test whose subject is a repository artifact rather than a running
# behaviour: it reads the tree, a registry, a compose file or a doc as data and asserts a rule over it. The
# category is defined by that MECHANISM, never by subject. Its home is
# `api/tests/Unit/Gate/`, whose leaf names the CATEGORY. It was `tests/Unit/Shared/Architecture`
# until the name was wrong twice over: `Architecture` named the folder's founding member — a
# bounded-context isolation gate — and never tracked what landed on it, while `Shared/` claimed a
# bounded context for gates that sweep every one of them.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Thirteen `php.lint.*` targets select gate classes by `--filter` on class name;
# twelve of them select out of the home and one selects a gate filed deliberately elsewhere. No rule
# anywhere said which case a new gate was in, so the placement was settled by whichever neighbour got
# copied — and one subject ended up in three places at once: the home, mirrored on a module, and a second
# one-file `tests/Functional/Gate/`. A convention that every build depends on and nothing
# states is not a convention, it is a habit.
#
# HOW TO DECIDE, for the line you are about to add: ask whose rule the gate enforces. A rule that belongs
# to ONE module — that module's own contract, whatever breadth the sweep needs to enforce it — may be
# filed `mirrored` on that module. A rule that belongs to no single module (a repo-wide convention, a
# registry at the api root, a compose file, a doc, the migration directory) goes in the home. Both
# placements are legitimate; what is not legitimate is choosing by imitation.
#
# Format, one per line. `::` is the field separator, so no field may contain it:
#   <path> :: home
#   <path> :: mirrored :: <directory under src/ or tests/ that the file mirrors>
#   <path> :: undecided :: <why the classification is open>
#   <path> :: functional :: <what it needs to run>
#
# `<path>` is api-relative. A `mirrored` subject must EXIST and the file must sit at its mirror
# (`tests/Unit/<subject minus its src/ or tests/ root>/…`) — `tests/` is the second mirror axis, for a gate
# whose subject is test infrastructure — and a bare `src`/`tests` is refused, since a whole source root is
# not a module. `undecided` is for a file already in the home whose membership in the category is an open
# question; it states a reason, because an exemption with no reason is indistinguishable from an oversight.
#
# `functional` is the fourth, and it answers a different question from the other three. They each assert a
# PLACEMENT; this one says the file is outside the CATEGORY — the shape heuristic matched it, but its subject
# is a running behaviour rather than an artifact read as data, which is the definition at the top of this
# file. Its placement under `tests/Functional/` is not in question, so it must NOT sit in the home, and the
# reason field says what it needs to run. `undecided` was the near miss and is deliberately not widened to
# cover it: answering two different questions with one token would make both unreadable.
#
# The gate is three classes — ArtifactGatePlacementGateTest over the real tree, plus
# ArtifactGateDetectionRulesGateTest and ArtifactGatePlacementRulesGateTest for the falsifiability of the
# detector and of the placement rules against synthetic input — run by `make php.lint.gate-placement`, each
# selected by exact name in its own run so a vanished class is an empty suite rather than a green subset.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it deliberately does NOT do. Read this list as the specification of the gate's blind spots: a green
# build proves NOTHING that is listed below. Every entry was measured by an adversarial pass that broke the
# first version of this gate, and the first entry is the one that matters — the list is not exhaustive,
# because a text matcher over an open-ended set of idioms cannot be shown to be.
#
#   - It never judges a classification. `mirrored` on a module whose rule the gate does not enforce passes,
#     and so does `home` for something that belongs on a module. The completeness check forces the decision
#     into a diff; review is what judges it. That is the trade every registry here makes.
#   - The universe has two halves and only one is airtight. Every test file under the home is in it by
#     LOCATION, whatever the file contains. Elsewhere it is a shape heuristic — kernel-free, crediting no
#     production coverage, and reaching a file outside its own directory — and it errs in BOTH directions.
#   - It under-approximates. The reach it recognises is a climb (`dirname(__DIR__…)`, `__DIR__ . '/..`, the
#     `__FILE__` spellings), a process-derived root (`getcwd()`, `realpath()`), reflection (`getFileName()`)
#     or an import of a rule engine. A root built from a path CONSTANT, from `$_SERVER`, or by concatenating
#     `DIRECTORY_SEPARATOR` is invisible — measured, a `Finder` rooted at a class constant passes as a
#     non-gate. So is a class whose `extends TestCase` arrives through an intermediate abstract base (a text
#     matcher cannot follow one; nothing in the tree has one today). A gate this half never matched has no
#     line to go stale, which is how one sat unclassified through the first green build of this gate.
#   - The `TestCase` import and `#[CoversClass]` target are read per statement, not per token: a grouped
#     import (`use PHPUnit\Framework\{TestCase};`) is invisible to both. `php.lint.event-bus` paid for this
#     exact class of gap with `token_get_all`; this detector does not. The window is narrow rather than open —
#     `@Symfony`/`@PhpCsFixer` (`api/tools/ecs/.php-cs-fixer.dist.php`) split grouped imports back to one per
#     statement, `php.cs-fixer` runs before this gate in both `php.quality` and `php.quality.dry-run`, and
#     CI's `php.cs.dry-run` refuses a PR still carrying one — so the only reachable miss is a gate authored
#     with a grouped import and checked locally before a full `php.quality` run normalises it.
#   - It over-approximates. An import of anything in `api/tests/Support/` is read as reaching the tree, so a
#     plain unit test that merely uses a helper from there joins the universe and needs a line. That is
#     deliberate — the engines' own unit tests are exactly the files whose placement is in question — but it
#     is not the definition at the top of this file.
#   - A gate that also `#[CoversClass]`es the production class it reads is skipped outside the home. That is
#     deliberate: such a test is not wholly a gate, and its placement follows the subject it covers.
#     Measured, sixteen tests sit in that overlap today, every one already mirrored on an existing `src/`
#     directory. Coverage credited through a docblock `@covers` is not read at all (the source is
#     comment-stripped first); the tree carries none.
#   - A gate that must boot a kernel is out of the category, because the category is kernel-free. Two
#     exist (`tests/Functional/Gate/SchedulerCheckpointDurabilityGateTest.php`,
#     `tests/Functional/Shared/Monitoring/ApiClientErrorBufferCouplingGateTest.php`) and this file says
#     nothing about where functional tests are filed.
#   - The sweep does not descend into a SYMLINKED directory under `tests/`, so a gate placed behind one is
#     invisible in both directions. Following links would open a loop hazard; nothing in the tree uses one.
#   - It reads text, not an AST. A heredoc holding gate-shaped source reads as the gate itself, and an
#     import aliased with `as` is resolved only for `TestCase`, not for a `#[CoversClass]` target.
#   - The engine ratchet reads ONE directory (`tests/Unit/Gate/Support/`). An engine filed
#     under a third name elsewhere passes; a rename of that directory is caught only because emptying it is
#     allowed solely when both engines are found in `api/tests/Support/`.
#   - A `mirrored` subject under `tests/Unit/` has no expressible mirror — the derivation prefixes
#     `tests/Unit/` to what is left of the subject — so such a line reds rather than being satisfiable.
#   - The reader skips only lines STARTING with `#`, so a trailing comment is swallowed into the last field
#     and reported as a bad subject rather than as a comment. Every registry in this repo shares that reader.
#   - Nothing here proves a gate ASSERTS anything. A registered class whose every assertion is vacuous is
#     classified exactly like one that works.
#   - Nothing here reads the make targets. A gate wired into no target still needs a line and still gets
#     one; whether a target runs it is pinned by `failOnEmptyTestSuite`, which turns a `--filter` matching
#     nothing into exit 1 the next time that target runs, and says nothing about one matching a subset.
#
# What the two directions do close: a gate that HAS a line cannot silently lose it. A detector that stops
# seeing something turns its line stale, and one that starts seeing more leaves the newcomer unclassified.
# A gate the shape half never matched is invisible to both, which is the hole above and not a guarantee.
#
# The two engine tests in the home (BehatVocabularyReaderTest, StepVocabularyRegistryTest) are `home`
# because their engines are; they move to `tests/Unit/Support/ :: mirrored :: tests/Support` on the day the
# engines move, which is the placement PhpSourceTest already has.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

